title: Old style mod files are better.
pid: eadb37e1-7b61-416c-8696-7c093e2613b3
body: |2-
    The 'old style' is, for every source folder '(module)/'
    with source files '(module)/(filename).rs' you want in your program,
    to create a 'mod file' '(module)/mod.rs'
    listing them.

    The 'new way' is to instead make a file '(module).rs'
    in the same folder as '(module)/',
    and use it to indicate which files in '(module)/' to include.

    After using the new way a while, I now know I prefer the old way.
    That's because it lets me visually distinguish between two cases:
    - I use only a mod file if the files it refers to
      are used by scattered users.
    - I use both a '(module).rs' file and a '(module)/mod.rs' file
      if the former actually includes interesting code
      where the modules referred to by the latter come together.

    In the new style, where the same name '(module).rs'
    is likely to be a boring list of 'use' statements
    but might include interesting function definitions,
    I am likely to overlook it.
